//
// Conduit (--use-conduit + --conduit-fuse-spatial + --conduit-fuse-core-
// bodies-flag + --conduit-fuse-channels-flag) end-to-end run for the
// HYBRID Conduit fusion pipeline — exercises ALL THREE Conduit fusion
// passes simultaneously on a SwiGLU-shaped 2:1 fan-in chained into a
// downstream sink with two parallel external outputs.
//
// Why this fixture exists: this is the actual Llama-decode hybrid pipeline
// shape (convergent SwiGLU + chained downstream + co-located producer-side
// outputs).  If this passes HW, fused-decode capability is HW-confirmed at
// SMALL BLAST RADIUS before Llama-scale verify — failures here isolate to
// pass-pair / pass-triple interaction surface that single-pass smokes
// (fuse_operators_convergent_npu / fuse_core_bodies_npu /
// fuse_channels_npu) cannot catch even when each individually passes.
//
// Sibling references:
//   * `test/npu-xrt/fuse_operators_convergent_npu/conduit.lit` — K=2
//     convergent merge component (SwiGLU gate+up→mul fan-in) verified in
//     isolation with --conduit-fuse-spatial only.  Hybrid fixture builds
//     on the same fusion_group="swiglu_fg0", fusion_index=0/1 pairing.
//   * `test/npu-xrt/fuse_core_bodies_npu/conduit.lit` — core-body loop
//     fusion component (Add → Mul 1:1 chain) verified in isolation with
//     --conduit-fuse-core-bodies-flag --conduit-fuse-spatial.  Hybrid
//     fixture extends the pattern: mul → sink chain is merged purely by
//     aie-combine-device{same-tile=true} (gated by --conduit-fuse-core-
//     bodies-flag) — both cores already live on tile(0,2) so no
//     additional fusion-group annotation is needed (and adding one
//     would trigger --conduit-fuse-operators's mixed convergent + 1:1
//     scope-out; locked Q2 2026-04-26).
//   * `test/npu-xrt/fuse_channels_npu/conduit.lit` — channel annotation
//     pass component (single-device producer-side same-tile grouping)
//     verified in isolation with --conduit-fuse-channels-flag.  Hybrid
//     fixture creates the channel-fusion candidate pair (ext_out_a +
//     ext_out_b) as the post-fusion residual on the merged mul+sink tile.
//   * `test/npu-xrt/fuse_operators_basic_npu/conduit.lit` — 1:1 spatial
//     baseline (Add → Mul) verified with --conduit-fuse-spatial only.
//
// Pre-fuse: 4 separate `aie.device` blocks (devGate, devUp, devMul,
// devSink) all on tile(0,2) of column 0.
//
// Pipeline composition (under all three flags):
//   1. --conduit-fuse-spatial → --conduit-fuse-operators:
//      K=2 convergent merge: devGate's @inter_gate + devUp's @inter_up
//      + devMul's @consume_gate + @consume_up (all sharing fusion_group=
//      "swiglu_fg0", paired by fusion_index=0/1) collapse to two
//      @fused_intermediate_N channels (per locked Track 3 design;
//      consumer-side stays single-producer per-channel).
//      The devMul → devSink 1:1 leg is intentionally NOT annotated for
//      --conduit-fuse-operators.  Mixing convergent + 1:1 fusion-group
//      modes is a deliberate scope-out (locked Q2 design 2026-04-26;
//      rejection pinned by
//      test/Dialect/Conduit/fuse_operators_convergent_mixed_fusion_groups_BUG.mlir).
//      The 1:1 mul→sink merge is achieved by step 2 below instead.
//   2. --conduit-fuse-core-bodies-flag → routes --aie-combine-device with
//      same-tile=true (per fuse_core_bodies_npu/conduit.lit).  Because
//      both devMul and devSink already place their cores on tile(0,2),
//      combine-device merges the two devices; core-body fusion then
//      merges their per-iteration loop bodies into one core, and the
//      @mul_inter / @consume_mul intermediate is erased.  Per
//      fuse_core_bodies_npu, same-tile=true is required for core-body
//      merging — without the flag, spatial fuse alone routes combine-
//      device with same-tile=false and scatters cores across columns,
//      preventing core-body fusion entirely.
//   3. --conduit-fuse-channels-flag → fuse-channels (annotation-only)
//      walks producer-side conduits on the same tile in the same parent
//      block with disjoint live windows.  The merged mul+sink core
//      produces TWO external outputs (@ext_out_a + @ext_out_b) on the
//      SAME tile in the SAME parent block (the inner scf.for body),
//      sequenced so the A-section release happens before the B-section
//      acquire — disjoint windows, both depth=1.  Pass C consumes the
//      stamped `dma_channel_group="groupN"` + `fuse_mode="static"`
//      annotations to fold the grouped conduits onto a single HW DMA
//      channel.  IR shape is otherwise unchanged by fuse-channels itself
//      — the annotation drives Pass C downstream.
//
// Expected post-spatial-fuse placement under npu2_4col budget (column-
// spread for the K=2 producers per fuse_operators_convergent_npu
// behavior, while same-tile=true keeps the mul+sink chain co-located):
//   col 0 tile(0,2): gate producer
//   col 1 tile(0,2): up producer
//   col 2 tile(0,2): merged mul+sink (post core-body fuse)
//
// PASS expectation: every dispatch (4 invocations) completes; output
// bytes match reference computed by test.cpp:
//   gate_in[j] = up_in[j] = (j%8)
//   ext_out_a[j] = (gate*up) + 1.0
//   ext_out_b[j] = (gate*up) + 2.0
// All bf16-exact.  Symmetric inputs make the producer-side arg-
// reprojection order irrelevant; distinct +1/+2 sink constants surface
// channel-fusion mis-routing as a byte mismatch (vs silent passthrough
// if both outputs computed the same value).
//
// Failure-mode interpretation (specific to the 3-pass interaction
// surface that single-pass smokes cannot catch):
//   * Convergent-merge bug → wrong-bytes on BOTH out_a and out_b
//     (gate/up producer-side intermediates mis-paired during
//     fused_intermediate_N erasure; both downstream sinks see corrupted
//     mul values).  Sibling fuse_operators_convergent_npu in isolation
//     would catch this, but PAIRWISE interaction with core-body fusion
//     can cause downstream mul_inter erase to mis-thread halves.
//   * Core-body merge interaction bug → hang on the SECOND invocation
//     (Pattern E forward-chain mis-fusion class; merged mul+sink core
//     wedges across reuse) OR truncated output from iter-count mis-
//     alignment.  Sibling fuse_core_bodies_npu would catch the
//     standalone class; this fixture catches the case where convergent-
//     merge upstream emits fused_intermediate_N consumer-side IR that
//     the merged mul+sink core mis-handles per-iteration.
//   * Channel-grouping race → out_a and out_b SWAPPED in the output
//     (mis-routed across the grouped HW DMA channel) OR all-zero on one
//     of the two outputs (BD-ring ordering violation post-grouping).
//     Sibling fuse_channels_npu catches the standalone case; this
//     fixture catches the case where the channel-fusion candidates are
//     produced by a CORE-BODY-FUSED merged core (i.e., the producer-
//     side same-tile pair only EXISTS because of the upstream fusions).
// PASS = ALL THREE pass interactions correct simultaneously.  This is
// the strongest single-fixture signal short of a full Llama compile.
//
// HW-verify status: this fixture cannot HW-verify cleanly until #92
// lands (PassC self-loop depth>1 producer rotation counter not allocated;
// currently breaks fuse-channels HW).  Authoring it now so it is
// LIT-ready and can be HW-verified the moment #92 closes.  Lit-only
// parse + 3-file structural parity check is what is verifiable today.
//
// No stateful companion: the source IR uses 4 independent `aie.device`
// blocks linked only by `fusion_group` annotations + Conduit-only
// channel-fusion annotation semantics — stateful objectfifo lowering
// does not span devices and has no `dma_channel_group` equivalent, so a
// stateful run is structurally inapplicable here.  Reference is
// computed in test.cpp.
//
// REQUIRES: ryzen_ai, peano
//
// Kernel compute is inlined bf16 multiply + add.  Chess (xchesscc)
// cannot select `fp_to_bf16` for this inline core (`fatal error: error
// in backend: Cannot select: i32 = fp_to_bf16`); Peano can.  Use
// `--no-xchesscc --no-xbridge` to route the kernel compile through
// Peano (sibling pattern: `fuse_operators_convergent_npu/conduit.lit`,
// `fuse_core_bodies_npu/conduit.lit`, `fuse_channels_npu/conduit.lit`,
// `loadpdi/run.lit`, `vec_vec_add_objfifo_init/aie2.py`).
//
// Reference is independently authored by %S/gen_reference.py (numpy,
// derived from the spec text — closes "validation gap 2: independently-
// authored reference").  Two regimes run end-to-end:
//   symmetric  — gate[j] = up[j] = (j%8)              (legacy coverage)
//   asymmetric — gate[j] = (j%8), up[j] = (j%8)+1     (closes "validation
//                                                      gap 3: gate/up
//                                                      arg-mis-pairing
//                                                      blind spot")
// gen_reference.py writes 4 .bin files per regime (gate_in, up_in,
// expected_a, expected_b — bf16 raw bits) into the per-test CWD.  The
// test binary then loads the inputs + expected via --gate-in / --up-in /
// --expected-a / --expected-b CLI args and byte-compares HW output to
// the on-disk reference per invocation.
//
// RUN: cp %S/aie.mlir aie_arch.mlir
// RUN: %run_on_npu2% sed 's/NPUDEVICE/npu2_4col/g' -i aie_arch.mlir
// RUN: %run_on_npu2% %python aiecc.py --no-aiesim --no-xchesscc --no-xbridge --aie-generate-xclbin --aie-generate-npu-insts --no-compile-host --use-conduit --conduit-fuse-spatial --conduit-fuse-core-bodies-flag --conduit-fuse-channels-flag --xclbin-name=aie.xclbin --npu-insts-name=insts.bin aie_arch.mlir
// RUN: %run_on_npu2% clang %S/test.cpp -o test.exe -std=c++17 -Wall %xrt_flags -lrt -lstdc++ %test_utils_flags
// RUN: %run_on_npu2% %python %S/gen_reference.py --regime symmetric --out-dir .
// RUN: %run_on_npu2% ./test.exe -x aie.xclbin -k MLIR_AIE -i insts.bin --gate-in gate_in_sym.bin --up-in up_in_sym.bin --expected-a expected_a_sym.bin --expected-b expected_b_sym.bin
// RUN: %run_on_npu2% %python %S/gen_reference.py --regime asymmetric --out-dir .
// RUN: %run_on_npu2% ./test.exe -x aie.xclbin -k MLIR_AIE -i insts.bin --gate-in gate_in_asym.bin --up-in up_in_asym.bin --expected-a expected_a_asym.bin --expected-b expected_b_asym.bin
